  "notes": "The one Bay entry whose harmony is allowed to move. The research is specific: they used live music and instrumentation rather than sampling in the 90s, so the harmony is played and can genuinely change mid-song with minor 7th/9th colour and real modulations. That is why this model has four-chord functional families and a wider key list than the g-funk parent — the extra keys stand in for the modulation the engine has no other way to express, and that substitution is named here rather than hidden. \"Mordant, bass-driven funk\" is the second finding and it makes the bass the design target: `followRootsProb` 0.28 with a register reaching down to D1. His own stated distinction — producer rather than beat maker, arrangement serving the vocal's emotional intensity — becomes a high `switchUpProb` and a high `dropOutBeats.prob`, so layers arrive and leave across the record instead of a static loop repeating. Live percussion on top of MPC programming is four perc lanes at 2–4 a bar. ⚠ `medium` per the research's medium-high; the method is quoted, the tempo band and the chord families are read from the catalog rather than from him. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 46 to 45 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 46 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 1.",
